is: **For the range of human activity from sedentary to very high (but not extreme ultra-endurance), more use does not cause more structural degradation; it causes less.** If I’m wrong, you would be able to show me clear evidence that, for example: - Moderate lifelong exercisers have *more* myocardial fibrosis than sedentary people of the same age. - Moderate exercisers develop heart failure *earlier* or *more often* than sedentary controls. - The hearts of fit people show *worse* diastolic function (stiffness) with age than unfit people. - Valve calcification progresses *faster* in people with lower resting heart rates and higher cardiac output from exercise. - The age-related loss of maximum heart rate is *worse* in athletes than in couch potatoes. If any of those were true, my argument would collapse instantly. But the evidence shows the precise opposite on every single point. The couch potato’s heart has more fibrosis, more stiffness, worse diastolic function, worse valve disease, and earlier heart failure than the active person’s heart. If “use” were the primary driver of deterioration, that would be impossible. The fact that it’s not only possible but consistently observed is the strongest evidence that I’m not just playing word games. ### 3. The “Relabeling” Is Actually a Crucial Distinction When you say valve calcification is “wear,” and I say it’s “disease,” we’re both looking at the same stiff, bony valve leaflet. The difference is what we think caused it. If it’s just wear from opening and closing, then every heartbeat brings it closer, and a marathoner’s valve should be stiffer than a sedentary person’s. But it’s not. So the cause can’t be the number of cycles. It must be something else: the metabolic environment, the blood pressure, the cholesterol, the inflammation. Those are modifiable, and exercise improves them. That’s not relabeling to avoid admitting wear; it’s identifying the true causal factor so we can do something about it. ### 4. The One Place Where “Wear” Appears Is the Exception That Proves the Rule I admitted that at the extreme end of lifelong ultra-endurance, a small amount of fibrosis and AFib risk appears that seems directly related to the volume of exercise. That is the one place where “use” may cause direct structural change. And it is the exception that proves the rule because it only happens at a volume that almost no one reaches, and even then, the heart remains functionally superior and the person lives longer. If the wear model were correct, that deterioration would start at the low end and gradually increase. Instead, it’s absent for 99% of the range and only appears at the very edge. That’s not a sliding scale; it’s a threshold effect, which is a completely different biological principle. ### 5. Am I Unfalsifiable? No, I’m Precise If I were truly unfalsifiable, I would say: “The heart never deteriorates from use under any circumstances, and any evidence you bring is just disease.” But I haven’t said that. I’ve said: - Extreme use can cause specific, limited issues (fibrosis, AFib). - Age-related changes happen, but they are not primarily driven by the mechanical act of pumping, and they are slower and less harmful in active hearts. - The functional failure of the heart—the kind that kills people—is overwhelmingly caused by diseases that exercise prevents, not by the accumulated cycles of a healthy pump. You can absolutely falsify that by showing that, head-to-head, active hearts fail earlier or harder than inactive ones at the same age. You can’t, because the data doesn’t exist. My argument is testable. It’s just that the tests keep coming back in its favor. You’ve pushed me to the very edge of what logic can clarify. You’re right that biology is messy, and the line between aging, wear, and disease is sometimes blurry. But it’s not imaginary. The fact that the heart of a lifelong runner is biologically younger than a sedentary peer’s, despite billions more hard beats, is a fact so stubborn that it forces us to abandon the simple mechanical model. That’s not me relabeling. That’s the heart telling us, in its own language, that use is not its enemy.
